import Foundation

// MARK: - Theme Content Batch 3: Healing Relationships & Hearing God's Voice

extension ContentLibrary {

    // MARK: - Healing Relationships (8 entries)

    static let healingRelationshipsContent: [DayContent] = [
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Matthew 5:23-24",
            scriptureText: "Therefore, if you are offering your gift at the altar and there remember that your brother or sister has something against you, leave your gift there in front of the altar. First go and be reconciled to them; then come and offer your gift.",
            devotionalTitle: "Reconciliation Before Religion",
            devotionalText: "Jesus interrupts worship for the sake of relationship. The offering on the altar can wait. If you remember that someone has something against you—not that you have something against them, but that they feel wronged by you—stop and go make it right. Jesus places relationship repair above religious practice. This is stunning, and it reveals how much God values the health of human relationships. Worship means less if the people around you are wounded by your neglect.",
            prayerText: "Lord, give me the courage to step away from my comfort and go to the person I have wounded. Soften my heart and theirs, and let reconciliation begin today. Help me to value relationships the way You do. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Is there someone who has something against you that you've been avoiding addressing?",
            actionSteps: [
                "Identify one person you need to reach out to for reconciliation",
                "Write out what you want to say before you say it, focusing on your part—not theirs"
            ]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Ephesians 4:31-32",
            scriptureText: "Get rid of all bitterness, rage and anger, brawling and slander, along with every form of malice. Be kind and compassionate to one another, forgiving each other, just as in Christ God forgave you.",
            devotionalTitle: "The Root Under the Surface",
            devotionalText: "Paul lists a progression that starts underground: bitterness, then rage, then anger, then slander, then malice. Most relational damage is not caused by spontaneous cruelty—it grows from unprocessed bitterness that festers over time. The command is not 'feel less bitter' but 'get rid of' it—active, deliberate removal. And the foundation for doing so is not willpower: it is the forgiveness you yourself have received in Christ. You forgive because you have been forgiven first.",
            prayerText: "Father, I confess the bitterness I have allowed to take root in my heart. Uproot it by Your grace and replace it with the compassion and kindness You have shown me. Teach me to forgive as freely as I have been forgiven. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Is there bitterness underneath the surface of a relationship that you haven't acknowledged?",
            actionSteps: [
                "Write honestly about a relationship where bitterness has taken root",
                "Pray: 'God, I forgive [name] the way You have forgiven me'—and mean it as a choice, not a feeling"
            ]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Matthew 18:15",
            scriptureText: "If your brother or sister sins against you, go and point out their fault, just between the two of you. If they listen to you, you have won them over.",
            devotionalTitle: "Go Directly",
            devotionalText: "Jesus's instruction for conflict is almost universally ignored: go directly to the person. Not to a mutual friend, not to social media, not to your journal in a way that rehearses grievance—to the person. The goal is not to win; it is to win them over. Reconciliation-focused confrontation sounds different from verdict-focused confrontation. One comes with an open hand, the other with a gavel. Jesus says: go, privately, with the goal of restoration.",
            prayerText: "Jesus, give me the bravery to go directly to the person I need to speak with. Guard my mouth from gossip and my heart from pride. Let my goal be restoration, not victory. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Is there a conflict you've been managing sideways—talking about instead of to the person involved?",
            actionSteps: [
                "Commit to going directly to the person you've been avoiding",
                "Practice the conversation beforehand with a trusted friend who will help you stay solution-focused"
            ]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Colossians 3:13",
            scriptureText: "Bear with each other and forgive one another if any of you has a grievance against someone. Forgive as the Lord forgave you.",
            devotionalTitle: "Bear With",
            devotionalText: "Forgiveness is the headline, but Paul includes something that often gets missed: bear with each other. That word means to put up with, to endure, to carry the weight of someone else's humanity. Not every relational difficulty requires a confrontation or a formal forgiveness transaction. Sometimes it requires the quiet, daily work of choosing to stay—to bear with someone's rough edges, their slow growth, their limitations. This is not weakness; it is one of the most difficult forms of love.",
            prayerText: "Lord, grant me the patience to bear with those who are difficult to love. Help me see their humanity the way You see mine. Where I want to correct, teach me to endure with grace. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Is there someone in your life you need to 'bear with' rather than fix or correct?",
            actionSteps: [
                "Choose one difficult person in your life and pray for them by name for 5 minutes",
                "Identify one irritating quality in that person and ask God to help you see it with compassion"
            ]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Luke 6:27-28",
            scriptureText: "But to you who are listening I say: Love your enemies, do good to those who hate you, bless those who curse you, pray for those who mistreat you.",
            devotionalTitle: "The Hardest Assignment",
            devotionalText: "This command is so demanding that people often spiritualize it into irrelevance. But Jesus gives it four concrete forms: love, do good, bless, pray. Not feel warmth, not forgive in your heart alone—active verbs, directed toward the person who hurt you. The research on this is now catching up to what Jesus knew: praying specifically for someone who wronged you is one of the most effective means of releasing your own resentment. Enemies are the hardest people to love. They are also, Jesus says, your assignment.",
            prayerText: "God, I bring before You the person who has hurt me most. I choose to bless them and not curse them. Release me from resentment and fill me with Your love for them, even when it feels impossible. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Who is your 'enemy'—the person who has hurt you most—and what would it mean to pray for them today?",
            actionSteps: [
                "Pray for the person who has hurt you most—specifically, for their wellbeing and not their punishment",
                "Do one concrete kind thing for someone who has wronged you, without telling anyone"
            ]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Romans 12:18",
            scriptureText: "If it is possible, as far as it depends on you, live at peace with everyone.",
            devotionalTitle: "Your Side of the Street",
            devotionalText: "Paul's language is carefully bounded: 'if it is possible' and 'as far as it depends on you.' Peace is not always achievable. Some relationships are unsafe, some people are not willing. You cannot control the other person. What you can control is your own choices, your own words, your own posture. This verse releases you from the burden of engineering reconciliation—and calls you to the work of managing what is actually yours to manage. Clean up your side of the street.",
            prayerText: "Father, show me what is mine to own in my broken relationships. Free me from trying to control others, and give me the humility to clean up my own side of the street. Let me be an instrument of Your peace. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "In a strained relationship, what is your side of the street? What are you responsible for?",
            actionSteps: [
                "Write honestly about your contribution to a broken relationship—without mentioning the other person's part",
                "Take one concrete step that is within your control toward peace in that relationship"
            ]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Genesis 50:20",
            scriptureText: "You intended to harm me, but God intended it for good to accomplish what is now being done, the saving of many lives.",
            devotionalTitle: "Reframing the Wound",
            devotionalText: "Joseph said this to the brothers who had sold him into slavery. He did not minimize what they did—'you intended to harm me' is honest. But he held it alongside a larger frame: God's intention. This is not naive positivity or denial. It is the hard-won theological vision of someone who had walked through years of suffering and seen what God was building on the other side. You may not be able to see Joseph's perspective yet. But you can begin to ask: what might God be building here?",
            prayerText: "Lord, I trust that You can redeem even the deepest wounds in my life. Help me to see Your hand at work in what others meant for harm. Give me Joseph's vision to see beyond the pain to Your purpose. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Is there a wound in a relationship that you can begin to hold with the question: 'What might God be doing here?'",
            actionSteps: [
                "Write about a relational wound from the perspective of what God might have been doing",
                "Consider reaching out to Joseph's 'brothers' in your life—someone who hurt you—to extend grace"
            ]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "1 Corinthians 13:7",
            scriptureText: "It always protects, always trusts, always hopes, always perseveres.",
            devotionalTitle: "The Staying Kind of Love",
            devotionalText: "Paul's famous description of love ends with four 'always' statements that describe endurance: protects, trusts, hopes, perseveres. Real love is not an emotion that comes and goes—it is a practice that stays. Relationships heal slowly. Trust rebuilds in small increments. The love that heals relationships is the kind that keeps showing up even when the progress is invisible. Not naïve or codependent, but committed to the long arc of restoration.",
            prayerText: "God, when I am tempted to walk away, fill me with the persevering love that comes only from You. Teach me to protect, to trust, to hope, and to stay. Let Your love flow through me into the relationships that need healing most. Amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "In which relationship are you most tempted to give up on? What would persevering look like?",
            actionSteps: [
                "Recommit in prayer to one relationship you've been tempted to abandon",
                "Do one small, consistent thing this week to invest in a relationship that needs healing"
            ]
        )
    ]

    // MARK: - Hearing God's Voice (8 entries)

    static let hearingGodsVoiceContent: [DayContent] = [
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "John 10:27",
            scriptureText: "My sheep listen to my voice; I know them, and they follow me.",
            devotionalTitle: "You Were Made to Hear Him",
            devotionalText: "Jesus doesn't say His sheep struggle to hear His voice. He says they listen to it as a present, ongoing reality. Hearing God is not a mystical gift reserved for a spiritual elite—it is the normal experience of every sheep who belongs to the Shepherd. If you feel like you can't hear God, the problem is almost never that He has stopped speaking. It is usually that life has gotten too loud, or we have stopped expecting to hear anything. Lean in. His voice is there.",
            prayerText: "Good Shepherd, I am Your sheep and I want to hear Your voice. Quiet the noise around me and within me so that I can listen. Increase my expectation that You are speaking and my ability to recognize Your voice. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Do you expect to hear God's voice? What has shaped that expectation?",
            actionSteps: [
                "Set aside 10 minutes of quiet today with no agenda but to listen",
                "Write down any impression, verse, or thought that comes and hold it loosely"
            ]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "1 Kings 19:11-12",
            scriptureText: "The Lord said, 'Go out and stand on the mountain in the presence of the Lord, for the Lord is about to pass by.' Then a great and powerful wind tore the mountains apart and shattered the rocks before the Lord, but the Lord was not in the wind. After the wind there was an earthquake, but the Lord was not in the earthquake. After the earthquake came a fire, but the Lord was not in the fire. And after the fire came a gentle whisper.",
            devotionalTitle: "After the Noise",
            devotionalText: "Elijah expected God in the dramatic—wind, earthquake, fire. Powerful things, things that demand attention. God was not in any of them. He was in the gentle whisper that came after the spectacle had passed. We often wait for God to speak through thunderclaps while missing the quiet nudge, the settled peace, the soft word in the middle of an ordinary moment. Learning to hear God's voice often means developing a tolerance for quiet—and the expectation that something small might be significant.",
            prayerText: "Lord, train my ears to hear Your gentle whisper. Forgive me for looking for You only in the dramatic while missing Your still, small voice. Help me to be still and know that You are God. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Has God ever spoken to you in a way you almost missed because it was too quiet or ordinary?",
            actionSteps: [
                "Practice 5 minutes of complete silence today, with no podcast, music, or phone",
                "Journal about a time you heard God in an unexpected, quiet way"
            ]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Psalm 119:105",
            scriptureText: "Your word is a lamp for my feet, a light on my path.",
            devotionalTitle: "Scripture Is the Microphone",
            devotionalText: "A lamp for the feet is a close-range light—enough to see your next step, not the whole road. Scripture is the primary, tested, reliable way God speaks. Other impressions and promptings are real, but they are always subject to the test of what God has already said in His Word. If you want to hear God more clearly, the most direct path is to spend more time in Scripture—not as an academic exercise, but with the expectation that the living God is speaking through these words to you, right now.",
            prayerText: "Father, make Your Word come alive to me. As I open the Scriptures, open my heart to receive what You are saying. Let Your Word be a lamp to my feet and a light to my path today. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Has a scripture ever felt like it was written specifically for your current situation? What happened?",
            actionSteps: [
                "Read one psalm today slowly, pausing every few lines to ask: 'God, what are You saying to me here?'",
                "Write down one verse that has felt like direct guidance in the past and meditate on it again"
            ]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Isaiah 30:21",
            scriptureText: "Whether you turn to the right or to the left, your ears will hear a voice behind you, saying, 'This is the way; walk in it.'",
            devotionalTitle: "Guidance You Can Trust",
            devotionalText: "God promises active guidance: not just that He has a plan for you, but that He will speak it into your decisions. 'A voice behind you' suggests gentle direction—not overriding your will, but accompanying your steps and speaking when you're about to stray. This is God's posture toward you in your decisions: He is not withholding guidance to test your guessing ability. He wants you to walk in the right way more than you want to find it. Ask. Expect an answer.",
            prayerText: "God, I need Your guidance. Speak clearly into my decisions and help me to trust the direction You give. When I turn to the right or to the left, let me hear Your voice saying, 'This is the way.' In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Is there a decision you're facing where you need to hear God's direction? Have you asked?",
            actionSteps: [
                "Bring a specific decision to God in prayer and then sit and listen for 5 minutes",
                "Share what you're discerning with a trusted friend and ask for their perspective"
            ]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Romans 8:16",
            scriptureText: "The Spirit himself testifies with our spirit that we are children of God.",
            devotionalTitle: "The Inner Witness",
            devotionalText: "One of the primary ways God speaks is through the inner witness of His Spirit—that deep, settled conviction that something is true or right. This is not the same as emotion or wishful thinking. The Spirit's testimony has a quality of certainty that persists across emotional states. Learning to distinguish between your own desires and the Spirit's witness is a crucial skill, developed over time through Scripture, prayer, and community. You have the Spirit of God living in you. He speaks from the inside out.",
            prayerText: "Holy Spirit, sharpen my ability to recognize Your voice within me. Teach me to distinguish Your witness from my own emotions and desires. I thank You that You dwell in me and speak from the inside out—help me to listen well. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Have you experienced the inner witness of the Spirit? How did you recognize it as His voice?",
            actionSteps: [
                "Ask God to develop your awareness of the Spirit's voice in your inner life",
                "Journal about a decision where you had strong inner conviction and trace what happened"
            ]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Proverbs 3:5-6",
            scriptureText: "Trust in the Lord with all your heart and lean not on your own understanding; in all your ways submit to him, and he will make your paths straight.",
            devotionalTitle: "Submit, Then See",
            devotionalText: "The promise of straight paths follows a prerequisite: submit to Him in all your ways. Hearing God clearly is deeply connected to the posture of submission. When we have already decided what we want the answer to be, we stop genuinely listening—we start filtering for confirmation. God makes paths straight for the person who comes with open hands. Submission is not passive; it is the active decision to hold your plans loosely enough that He can redirect them.",
            prayerText: "Lord, I open my hands before You and release my grip on the outcomes I have been clinging to. I choose to trust You with all my heart and not lean on my own understanding. Make my paths straight as I submit my plans to You. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Are you approaching any current decision with the outcome already decided? What would open hands look like?",
            actionSteps: [
                "Identify a decision you're holding tightly and pray: 'God, I want Your will, not mine'",
                "Write down what you think you want God to say—and then ask if you're willing to hear something different"
            ]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Habakkuk 2:2",
            scriptureText: "Then the Lord replied: 'Write down the revelation and make it plain on tablets so that a herald may run with it.'",
            devotionalTitle: "Write It Down",
            devotionalText: "God instructs Habakkuk to write the revelation down and make it plain. There is something about writing that sharpens and clarifies what we hear. Many people find that journaling their prayers unlocks a new layer of hearing—as they write what they're thinking, a responsive voice emerges. Writing also creates accountability: you can return to what you sensed God was saying and test it over time. If God is speaking to you, the instruction is to write it down and run with it.",
            prayerText: "Father, as I write and reflect, speak to me with clarity. Help me to capture what You are saying so I can return to it and walk in it faithfully. Give me ears to hear and the discipline to record what I receive. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Do you have a habit of writing down what you sense God saying to you? What has been the result?",
            actionSteps: [
                "Write out a prayer today and then write beneath it: 'God's response:' and wait to see what comes",
                "Start or restart a spiritual journal this week, even just a few lines per day"
            ]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Jeremiah 33:3",
            scriptureText: "Call to me and I will answer you and tell you great and unsearchable things you do not know.",
            devotionalTitle: "The Invitation Is Always Open",
            devotionalText: "God issues an open invitation: call to Me and I will answer you. Not 'call to Me when you are spiritually mature enough,' not 'call to Me when you have been consistent in your quiet time.' Just call. And His answer will include great and unsearchable things—mysteries you couldn't discover on your own. The main barrier to hearing God is not His silence but our failure to call. He is ready and willing to speak to you today. You just have to ask.",
            prayerText: "God, I am calling out to You right now. I believe that You hear me and that You will answer. Reveal to me the great and unsearchable things that only You can show me. I am listening and ready to receive. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "What would you ask God right now if you truly believed He would answer?",
            actionSteps: [
                "Write down your most pressing question for God and bring it to Him in prayer today",
                "Return to that question over the next three days and write anything you sense in response"
            ]
        )
    ]
}
